<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mystras</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mistras</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mystras is a ruined medieval town on a spur of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-taygetus">Mount Taygetus</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin">William II of Villehardouin</a> built a castle there in 1249; ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> in 1262, it became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/despotate-of-the-morea">Despotate of the Morea</a> and a centre of late Byzantine art and learning, home of the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gemistos-plethon">Gemistos Plethon</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took it in 1460. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mystras.jpg" alt="Mystras" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Apaleutos25 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%9C%CF%85%CF%83%CF%84%CF%81%CE%AC%CF%82,_Mystras.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
